<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Raiatea</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1888 CE (approx.) · capital Uturoa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The sacred island kingdom of the Society Islands, home of the great marae of Taputapuatea, whose people fought French annexation until 1897.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Raiatea, called Havaiʻi in older traditions, was a religious and voyaging centre of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, and its marae of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taputapuatea">Taputapuatea</a> at Opoa drew chiefs from across the region. The cult of the war god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oro">ʻOro</a> spread from there. By the 18th century the high chiefs of the Tamatoa line ruled Raiatea and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahaa">Tahaʻa</a>, though warriors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bora-bora">Bora Bora</a> conquered the island around the 1760s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled through a chiefly hierarchy, with priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oro">ʻOro</a> wielding great influence. The priest-navigator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupaia">Tupaia</a>, who sailed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> in 1769, came from Raiatea. Missionaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> arrived in 1818, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread quickly, and the kingdom adopted written law codes. Breadfruit, coconut and fishing sustained the islanders, and Raiatea traded with visiting ships.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarnac-convention">Jarnac Convention</a> of 1847 between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> guaranteed the independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands-society-islands">Leeward Islands</a>. France annexed Raiatea in 1888, but a resistance led by the chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teraupoo">Teraupoʻo</a> lasted until 1897. The island became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-polynesia">French Polynesia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taputapuatea">Taputapuatea</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-raiatea.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Raiatea" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Bringing It All Back Home · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Raiatea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1888 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Uturoa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tamatoa VI</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Polynesian religion, later Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tahitian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Raiatea-Tahaa, Raiatea</dd></dl></aside></div>
